#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129034 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129215 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84167caa-9fa6-5162-a598-c2dd11d9ed85" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/id #uuid "fdf51620-58e9-5886-93e5-02a814b154bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8905" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=e^x$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=2.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=0$ to $x_2={2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\sqrt{1+({e}^{x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129035 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129216 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbff4737-2ea0-5d37-aa9e-395c4dc19b6a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/id #uuid "ae72af2a-ec7e-569d-89a8-550c8eeabdbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-8909" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ that lies between the lines $x=2$ and $x=4.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=2$ to $x_2=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x-2})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4(x-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4(x-2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}\\sqrt{\\frac{4x-7}{4(x-2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x-2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{4x-7}}{2\\sqrt{x-2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x-7}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=4x-7 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\textrm du = 4\\,\\textrm dx,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x-7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,|}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(27-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129036 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129217 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/id #uuid "fcfc9339-efd3-525f-a6f3-695124724e1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9120" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=y^3-y$ that lies in the second quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?\n\n![](images/1b/1bdbe21cbb517645dfe9362f3313c2d85118acfea1d839fe33ae9b9ac6c3ed50.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x = f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3}-y)=3{y}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1+{(3{y}^{2}-1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{2-6{y}^{2}+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129217 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "358a047c-5548-544e-bf1d-f9959f75de54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{4y+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430006} {:answer/id #uuid "6a63776c-a3e2-5252-b4f9-f985fc644009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430013} {:answer/id #uuid "75065807-d394-54ee-9589-a02778ac1b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{y(4y+1)}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430015} {:answer/id #uuid "92a3d043-6533-553c-a302-61c4dbd94b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4y+1}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430017} {:answer/id #uuid "f074a450-1ea5-5e8e-b2c1-3c16ad93c9d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}y\\sqrt{4y+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75065807-d394-54ee-9589-a02778ac1b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{y(4y+1)}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430015} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e573e19-5887-51da-bc0d-a02e833ab7a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "394bc722-72f5-5959-b491-d15c78d36cfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-109146" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{y}$ that lies between $y=1$ and $y=4.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=1$ to ${y}_{2}=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{y})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}y\\sqrt{\\frac{4y+1}{y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{y(4y+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129217 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93561cb9-31e8-5f50-b3bf-ac1e4c078018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430018} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7a244a-9ef8-56e4-ad31-1e3ad490a401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430020} {:answer/id #uuid "b0993602-e32e-5385-8615-215b677568b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430023} {:answer/id #uuid "c21c3926-fbd8-543d-9a89-059162436863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430024} {:answer/id #uuid "c6975ddc-3a08-5688-b3d1-0de4aad419be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6975ddc-3a08-5688-b3d1-0de4aad419be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430025} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd353f17-4f1a-5904-b62b-8c4277a7e856" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5eff5095-a0c0-5448-9e3d-e45ec11b1792" :question/math-academy-id "q-109186" :question/problem "![](images/a7/a70a12201e99ded8eed85b797c760862814e1f0a50129020fa6d3b35d4334394.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercepts (see figure above). What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0 \\\\ y & =\\pm n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129216 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbff4737-2ea0-5d37-aa9e-395c4dc19b6a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "422bd46c-effb-5d0b-904d-3ee35544d973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430009} {:answer/id #uuid "53183f8e-14d6-52c2-9f1a-70be683a7846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430011} {:answer/id #uuid "5f5eaa2f-7b67-59fb-ac74-96008538d1e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430012} {:answer/id #uuid "a548f782-4430-54c1-9ade-29c1fd806b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430021} {:answer/id #uuid "c743a931-9156-5119-af96-2e6b26ebadfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f5eaa2f-7b67-59fb-ac74-96008538d1e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430012} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8e6cbf3-7b66-5401-9a6d-5259b70ef31f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e501ffe3-f914-5273-b8d8-73d2e5bc3adb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109269" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2})=\\frac{1-x}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{1-2x+{x}^{2}}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1-2x+{x}^{2}}{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2})\\sqrt{\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}(3-x)}{3}(\\frac{x+1}{2\\sqrt{x}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(3-x)(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(3+2x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {[3x+{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129215 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84167caa-9fa6-5162-a598-c2dd11d9ed85" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "076d4f91-52ee-5265-9716-346105e7c17c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{4})\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430003} {:answer/id #uuid "32cf01df-92ff-5fdb-a4e6-5c90197c9574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{4})\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430005} {:answer/id #uuid "3a00d32e-d7d5-5b7b-ae72-6a9a030e3ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{4})\\sqrt{2-8{x}^{3}+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430007} {:answer/id #uuid "7f585402-a8df-55bb-b2ab-4ae33bb5202f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{(x-{x}^{4})}^{2}\\sqrt{2-8{x}^{3}+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430016} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a7b159-f3eb-5808-a9b3-9de83766c488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{4})\\sqrt{2-8{x}^{3}+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9a7b159-f3eb-5808-a9b3-9de83766c488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{4})\\sqrt{2-8{x}^{3}+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430032} :answer-field/id #uuid "59d98658-24fe-5433-bf3d-9f4ab64a837f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37b63c9c-6c92-5f44-bb20-4d23e2c51ff4" :question/math-academy-id "q-126523" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=x-{x}^{4}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x-{x}^{4})=1-4{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{4})\\sqrt{1+{(1-4{x}^{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{4})\\sqrt{2-8{x}^{3}+16{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129215 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84167caa-9fa6-5162-a598-c2dd11d9ed85" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51c26f7f-96ba-5d02-b426-27fbbc8d3851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{(2x-{x}^{2})}^{2}\\sqrt{5-8x+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430010} {:answer/id #uuid "71a555ef-03a3-57cb-9c25-d34ed5de385c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1-8x+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430014} {:answer/id #uuid "c95d3b99-6b88-52d5-97b3-f7bcc08f66b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x-{x}^{2})\\sqrt{2-2x+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430027} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ef6e77-7117-5a21-9fbc-23ba538a199d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(2x-{x}^{2})}^{2}\\sqrt{1-8x+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430029} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f8c068-5bb0-55aa-83b2-528951078bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\sqrt{5-8x+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4f8c068-5bb0-55aa-83b2-528951078bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\sqrt{5-8x+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430030} :answer-field/id #uuid "fab2c55e-07fe-5e9d-9f01-1f1219cae67d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d75b243-8939-5146-b57c-2c5e64292e74" :question/math-academy-id "q-126540" :question/problem "![](images/68/68a30adbe260a15c8514ceec2291da4be77af2335f8999d512ffd46c7b18c6bc.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=2x-{x}^{2}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(2-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-{x}^{2})=2-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+{(2-2x)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\sqrt{5-8x+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129216 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbff4737-2ea0-5d37-aa9e-395c4dc19b6a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cba0bba-3874-5f31-9506-90e69f99ee58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430004} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9acf2a-3897-5212-ae15-bafc3627e866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430008} {:answer/id #uuid "9643975e-56c0-5fb2-ba93-036c9d01f4c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430019} {:answer/id #uuid "a5fc830d-ddfb-52ef-95a5-7275134c641b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430022} {:answer/id #uuid "cd696a17-23bd-5ea8-84ec-8d71128b61db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5fc830d-ddfb-52ef-95a5-7275134c641b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430022} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6c197bd-bdee-548f-8f17-1f8666979616" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3f52ad3-220f-5e23-b87e-dca64195a02f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126580" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=1.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi x\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi (1-0) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
